WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.030
- It's my pleasure to welcome you today

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.320
to Machine Vision For Cultural Heritage

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.990
and Natural Science Collections,

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:18.000
a program of the
Yale-Smithsonian partnership.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.300
This is the first in what we hope

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.940
to be a number of study days

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.880
that are held either in New Haven,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.183
or in D.C. that sort
of bring together folks

00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.210
not just who are on the board

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.940
or who've been active in the
Yale-Smithsonian partnership,

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.790
but who are active in all
levels of the university

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.746
and in the institution respectively.

00:00:35.746 --> 00:00:38.640
The theme we've for this first study day,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.210
held here in Sterling Library

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.120
and the Franke Family Digital
Humanities Laboratory,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.047
is machine vision.

00:00:44.047 --> 00:00:46.700
And you can hear this
described as computer vision,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.880
you hear this described very broadly

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.130
as artificial intelligence,
neural networks,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.780
but we really wanted to focus on

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.870
the computational
analysis of Raster pixels.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.190
Especially coming from
an institution like Yale,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.410
which has such a strength of excellence

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.380
in linked open data, RDF
tuples, open access images,

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.990
we really wanted to push the boundary

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.410
and explore what would happen

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.230
if we actually started
analyzing the pixels

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.580
of some of our natural
heritage collections,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.100
in our cultural heritage
collections as well.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.700
And so, that was the reason we decided

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.040
to call this Machine Vision
for Cultural Heritage

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.870
in Natural Science Collections.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.804
We knew that both
institutions had world-famous

00:01:24.804 --> 00:01:27.900
natural science museums and institutions

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.090
of research and teaching,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.680
and we also knew about the
world-class collections

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.140
of art and other objects,
sculpture, history

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.860
that were present on both campuses

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.460
and both institutions.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.997
So, Machine Vision for Cultural Heritage

00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:41.550
and Natural Science Collections

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.421
unifies those resources,

00:01:43.421 --> 00:01:46.270
which are hundreds of years old,

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.332
and it puts that together
with some techniques

00:01:48.332 --> 00:01:49.880
which are very new,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.390
which have their origins perhaps

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.190
in the mid-century, the 20th century,

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.710
but which in the four
or five or six years,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.030
have really taken of as ways of analyzing

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.030
the visual data at scale.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.960
There's really no better way to

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.280
start us off than with
our first keynote speaker,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.010
who's Rebecca Dikow from
the data science lab

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.350
and the Office of the
Chief Information Officer

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.140
at the Smithsonian Institution.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.926
She'll be speaking today on
data-intensive approaches

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:17.680
to digitize museum collections.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.513
Rebecca?

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.640
- Thank you, Peter,
and thank you very much

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.373
for the invitation to be here.

00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:25.758
I'm happy to talk a little bit about

00:02:25.758 --> 00:02:28.690
some of the work we're doing
in the data science lab.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.030
I will preface it by saying,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.600
I'm a trained biologist

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.842
and came into the
high-performance computing world

00:02:35.842 --> 00:02:38.450
when I was generating genomics data

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.100
as part of my dissertation.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.620
And since I arrived at the Smithsonian,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.571
after my PhD in September
of 2012 as a postdoc,

00:02:46.571 --> 00:02:50.100
I start thinking about
Smithsonian collections

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.200
more broadly and not just genomic data.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.740
And so, today, I'll talk to you about

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.585
how we're expanding our big data research

00:02:56.585 --> 00:02:59.100
outside of biology,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.593
but I'll start with biology.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.690
So, the Smithsonian has
a number of challenges

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.609
to conducting large-scale
informatics research.

00:03:07.609 --> 00:03:10.743
First is there's a large
diversity of locations,

00:03:10.743 --> 00:03:15.480
heterogenous kinds of digital
data, incomplete meta data,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.440
and a lack of purpose-built
software tools.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.250
This is particularly true
for our biology groups

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.600
where most genomics
tools have been developed

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.150
around the human genome,

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.220
and the genomes that
Smithsonian scientists

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.860
are interest in are everything
but the human genome.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.583
So everything from clams to bacteria,

00:03:35.804 --> 00:03:38.851
bacteria are a little bit
easier than everything else,

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:43.851
to plants, to pretty much
anything you can imagine.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.520
And so we're constantly on
the lookout for new tools,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.103
and seeing how our data
fit into existing models.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.040
This isn't just a terribly resolved photo,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.160
it's a photo mosaic of an
aerial shot of the mall

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.400
from our archives

00:04:03.183 --> 00:04:05.500
that all of the pixels are filled with

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.960
other aerial shots and
other building photos

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.355
of the Smithsonian.

00:04:10.355 --> 00:04:13.780
for a machine vision workshop

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.200
that might be somewhat interesting.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.380
So the Smithsonian has 19 museums,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.440
9 research centers, and a zoo

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.840
most of which are on the Smithsonian Mall,

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.830
but some of which are very far-flung.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.980
We have the Smithsonian
Tropical Research Institute

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.490
in Panama, which has a large group of

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.403
very accomplished evolutionary
biologists, field biologists.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.710
The Smithsonian
Environmental Research Center

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.510
out in Annapolis, Maryland, as well,

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.170
who do ecological research.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.130
So, we're not just collections based,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.363
but research data based as well.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.410
When we talk about digitized collections,

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:54.030
I will keep a natural
history focus for now

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.030
just to to talk about the different

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.253
kinds of digitized data
that we're talking about.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.450
Photos, so for example,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.870
this is a photo of a pinned fly.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.820
The taxonomic names, specimen records,

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.410
genomic sequences,

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.240
so we have data in GenBank,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.903
geo-referenced localities,

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.073
and label data, transcribed
label data as well.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.920
I would say that these are all

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.230
part of the collections object,

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.650
but then there are other kinds of

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:30.770
auxiliary data that might
be part of our understanding

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:32.110
of this one specimen.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.510
Which could come from field books

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:38.280
where scientists who have
collected these specimens

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.770
wrote down some observations about it,

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.560
illustrations, again observations,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.260
you know, what was this fly eating

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.830
at the time it was collected,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.543
scientific publications,
and taxonomic descriptions.

00:05:53.708 --> 00:05:54.541
These all could be linked,

00:05:54.541 --> 00:05:57.363
these all are part of what
we know about this fly,

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.070
and being able to computer
cross all of these data

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.273
is something that we strive for.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.890
This is just an example of

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.590
what a specimen collecting
event looks like

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.620
in terms of where the data go.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.050
In the center this is Vicki Funk,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.277
she's the curator of botany

00:06:15.277 --> 00:06:17.900
and a great collaborator of mine.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.534
So, for example, she
went out to collect this

00:06:20.534 --> 00:06:24.090
purple very pretty flower,

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.840
the species is called
centrapalus pauciflorus.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.950
We decided we wanted
to sequence this genome

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:33.010
because it is a part in
the sunflower family tree

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.210
that has no genome around it,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.610
so we thought it would be interesting.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.270
She collected the seeds, actually,

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.120
and we grew it in a greenhouse

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.580
because we needed a lot of DNA

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.830
for the kind of DNA
sequencing we were doing.

00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:49.773
From the actual specimen,

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.690
most of the plant gets pressed,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.660
and so you can see an
image of a pressed plant.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.410
Some of it goes to a bio repository,

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.490
so we have its DNA forever.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.180
You might sequence a small piece of DNA,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.800
a barcode for plants, those
are chloroplast genes.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.840
Those data go to NCBI,
which is part of NIH,

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.963
it's generally called GenBank.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.475
The specimen information,

00:07:16.475 --> 00:07:18.500
there's the specimen record,

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:20.660
which is in the Natural History Museum

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.540
it goes to a database called Emu,

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.500
and those data are pushed to GBIF,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.120
the Global Biodiversity
Information Facility,

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.040
so that you can go to
GBIF and find records

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.260
of specimens from our museums

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.340
and also lots of other institutions.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.570
The photo might go to iDigBio.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.260
So, this is just one specimen,

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.680
and this all the places
that those data go to.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.860
It gets way more complicated.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.630
So, that's before any
research question, right?

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:51.060
So that's just like
we're going to document

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:51.980
that we collected this,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.771
and have put it in the
places it needs to go,

00:07:54.771 --> 00:07:58.700
but in order to do the research questions

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.520
Vicki's interested in,

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.370
we didn't wanna just sequence
the chloroplast gene.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.370
We wanted to sequence the entire genome.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:07.960
These are the different kind of parts

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.750
of research that we engage in.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.280
The little museum icons at the top show

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.630
in order to generate
the deep learning models

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.460
that Alex will talk about later,

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.800
we need data from lots
of different museums.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.650
We need specimen records,
locality information,

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.580
and images from all over the place.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.050
In order to build a phylogeny,

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.650
we need to sequence, in this case,

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:35.970
this is a paper we're about to publish

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.890
with 256 species in the sunflower family,

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:42.330
we sequenced 1,000 genes
for all those 256 species.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.320
So those data go to NCBI,

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.410
but we may, in some cases,

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.660
pull data from NCBI
from other institutions.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.030
And then, for the genomics,

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:54.690
there's a huge amount of software tools

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:58.037
that we need just to assemble
and annotate the genome.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:03.400
I'll show closer views of these.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.440
We'll make a genome browser

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.920
that we can share with our collaborators

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.670
where they can continue to
keep editing our annotation.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:11.210
Well, that's one genome.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.780
What about all the genomes that are being

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.120
generated at the Smithsonian?

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.940
I'll show something that's
under construction for us,

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.370
which is the Smithsonian Genome Hub

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.366
where Smithsonian researchers can

00:09:20.366 --> 00:09:23.120
put all their genomes in place,

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.570
and start thinking about
how to use them together.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:29.150
So, the research is what adds
a huge amount of complexity.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:32.260
Again, this is for one group of plants.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:36.050
There are hundreds of
Smithsonian researchers

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.513
that are kind of doing
things along these lines.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:44.173
I'll take a short little look at genomics.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.993
Genomics at the Smithsonian has been,

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:52.000
we're generally really
good at natural history.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.580
The scientists at the
Smithsonian know their organisms,

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:57.020
they know where to go in the
field to collect a species,

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.010
they know everything about it,

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.490
but genomics has been a big challenge

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:05.220
to get all the software
that researchers need

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.060
working well for their data.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.030
This is just an example
of some of the tools

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:12.693
that you need to assemble
a genome and annotate it.

00:10:13.851 --> 00:10:16.324
And a lot of times you
have to try multiple

00:10:16.324 --> 00:10:20.310
kinds of software to see
what works best for the data.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:22.640
This is just zooming
into the genome browser

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:23.590
that we're creating

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.540
where you have these prediction tracks.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:27.800
So, this is just zoomed into

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:30.170
a very small piece of one scaffold,

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:33.260
and all of the gene predictions on there,

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.027
which scientists can go in and say,

00:10:35.027 --> 00:10:39.650
"Okay, I know about genes
for drought tolerance."

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:41.360
I will go and look at those,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.150
and see in detail does that look right,

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:44.870
and how can I extract that,

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:48.933
and compare it to other
genomes that I know about.

00:10:50.603 --> 00:10:51.720
We're doing this in the cloud right now,

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.950
so we can easily share
it with other researchers

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.243
outside the Smithsonian.

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:00.050
So, we have a high-performance
computing cluster,

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:04.370
and part of my work is
helping to administer this.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:08.960
There are about 4,000 CPUs
and 26 total terabytes of RAM.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.440
That graph, the plot just shows

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.383
in the last 30 days the usage.

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:16.030
So, it is kind of up and down,

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.840
and it has a weekly cycle,

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:19.763
so people don't do as
much work on the weekends,

00:11:19.763 --> 00:11:21.183
which is good.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.260
But it's been really, really busy.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.180
We have 16 very high memory nodes.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.950
So, a genome assembly often you need

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.960
a terabyte or two terabytes of RAM

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:31.870
to assemble a genome.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:34.430
These are pretty beefy nodes.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.860
We have many high CPU nodes as well

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.690
because most of bioinformatic software

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:43.400
doesn't work well in NPI frameworks,

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.620
it's multi-threaded.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:49.430
For GPUs, we have a node with NVIDIA K80s,

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:51.320
but more recently we've, my team,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:56.150
has gotten two workstations
with NVIDIA GP100s,

00:11:56.150 --> 00:12:00.650
and we'll probably upgrade
the GPUs in Hydra very soon.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:03.290
We have more than 300
genomic software packages

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:04.520
installed on Hydra.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:05.810
This is just an ongoing thing

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.470
where people are requesting things.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.260
I don't know, maybe five times a week

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.440
we get requests for more software.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.390
And around 550 users.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:15.710
In terms of natural history,

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:17.772
that's about, I think, 182

00:12:17.772 --> 00:12:20.850
natural history museum users of Hydra.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:21.683
That's a lot.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.750
Hydra started at the
Smithsonian Astrophysical

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:27.830
Observatory, astrophysicists are way ahead

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.610
in terms of of high-performance computing,

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:30.510
but we're catching up.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.920
And so, we have as many biology users

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:34.140
as astrophysics user,

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.390
which is really a big change.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:37.440
It's really exciting.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.540
Training is a big part,
as well, of what we do

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:48.213
because we have hundreds of researchers,

00:12:48.213 --> 00:12:52.200
curators, postdoctoral
fellows, graduate fellows,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:56.100
interns that all wanna
do this kind of research.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.020
So Smithsonian has joined
The Carpentries organization.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:00.420
I'm sure many of you have heard of

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.980
data software and library carpentry.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:05.640
We joined, I think, two years ago,

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.810
and now we have 13 trained instructors

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:10.730
with, I think, 10 more that are just

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:14.463
finalizing their instructor training.

00:13:16.216 --> 00:13:17.049
In the past three years,

00:13:17.049 --> 00:13:19.000
we've trained more than 300 researches

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.528
in Python, R, genome analysis.

00:13:21.528 --> 00:13:24.260
We started doing more ad hoc workshops,

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.730
so people want to learn genome assembly,

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:31.010
so we would a six-week workshop

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.810
with an hour a week talking
about genome assembly,

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.260
but then we realized...

00:13:36.505 --> 00:13:37.720
We did a survey of Hydra users,

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:39.880
and said, "What kind of
workshops are you interested in?"

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.850
and it turned out that Python, R

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:47.850
very ground level workshops and techniques

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.200
that they can then apply
to all sort of research

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.360
were what people were
really interested in.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.890
We keep all our workshop
material on a GitHub page,

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.083
which you can go to.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:57.940
As part of our website,

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:00.729
we have a schedule and place where you can

00:14:00.729 --> 00:14:02.780
request workshops as well.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.050
So, our instructors would be happy

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:07.703
to go anywhere and give these workshops.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:11.710
Okay, and I'll talk a little about the

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:12.760
Biodiversity Genome Hub.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:13.947
It's under construction,

00:14:13.947 --> 00:14:16.263
but we're really excited about it.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.060
So, the plan is to have a place

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.040
that's accessible not just
within the Smithsonian,

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.170
but to our collaborators

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:25.890
where we can have all of
our genome data together.

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:28.330
So, it's even hard for
us at the Smithsonian

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:31.210
to list all the genome
projects that are going on.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:33.220
I've tried to get this information.

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:35.330
It's something like 150,

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.410
but having one place where we can bring

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:39.730
all the data together,

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.170
and have standards about how
people are storing that data,

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:43.670
and making sure it's backed up,

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.320
and making sure it's described in a way

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:49.200
that makes it reusable
is really important.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.360
So, we're constructing this website,

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.570
which will be run through Galaxy,

00:14:56.570 --> 00:15:00.353
which is a bioinformatics software.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.520
An opensource way to make reproducible

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:07.960
pipelines and workflows
of different tools,

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.260
as it wraps around any kind of

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.060
bioinformatics tool you'd want.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.100
And we hope to provide
researchers the ability

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.450
to do basic analytics on their
genomes once their sequenced

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:18.870
and share them with other people,

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:20.720
and hopefully allow students

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:24.330
and undergraduates and graduate students

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:29.330
to really get into genomics
in a not so intimidating way.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.700
So, now I'll talk more about
the computer visions stuff.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:35.700
Sorry for the detour,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:40.290
but I think the genomics work
is still pretty exciting.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.710
So, I'll give you four examples of

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:43.760
some of the work we're starting on.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:45.520
The botany project is the most mature,

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:47.830
and Alex will be talking
about that this afternoon,

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:52.140
and the other ones are just
still experiments at this point,

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:53.193
but pretty exciting.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.850
The Smithsonian
Digitization Program Office

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:01.890
has started a program in
the Natural History Museum

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:06.770
to digitize, or generate
images and transcriptions

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:10.000
of the labels for all
the botanical specimens

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:13.010
in the herbarium of which
there are five million.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.130
So, there's this conveyor belt

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:16.830
that runs eight hours a day,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.900
and generates thousands of images per day.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:20.630
I don't know exactly.

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.480
I think there are three
million at this point

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:23.430
that are completed.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:29.340
And as a first step, two
and half years ago or so,

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:33.697
we thought, "Okay, let's see if we can

00:16:33.697 --> 00:16:35.380
"train deep learning models

00:16:37.437 --> 00:16:41.140
"to classify, and to
detect mercury staining?"

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:42.620
Why mercury staining?

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:46.070
Well, botanists used
preserve their specimens

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:47.830
with mercury to keep pests away,

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:49.526
and we know now that's not

00:16:49.526 --> 00:16:53.020
a great thing to have alongside people.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:55.720
And you can see the specimen on the left

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.020
has been stained with mercury,

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:59.870
and over the years, the
mercury crystallizes out,

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.960
and so it's very visible to the human eye.

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.290
But it's not generally part of

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:05.883
the metadata for the specimen.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:09.900
Sometimes there's a little
poison stamp on some of them.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:13.210
We're not exactly sure if
that directly correlates

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:14.700
to whether it has mercury or not

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:17.130
or it could mean something else.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:20.360
But we can see it, it's
not part of the metadata,

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:22.480
so we thought an interesting question was,

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.120
well, can the computers see it,

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.890
and can we then point out
hotspots in the collection

00:17:26.890 --> 00:17:30.650
that might be, where some
mediation might be required.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.050
They put the mercury stained specimens

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:36.240
that they know about into special folders,

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:40.590
but no one person can
open five million folders

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.363
to check the rest of them.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.000
And then family ID.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.483
These are two different
families of fern allies.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:54.770
They're quite similar
to non-fern biologists,

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.040
such as myself (laughs),

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.940
and we thought we would do a very simple

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.560
binary classification problem.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.123
Can we tell these apart
with good accuracy?

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:09.063
And NVIDIA helped us with
this first pilot as well.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.960
So, these are just confusion matrices

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:17.530
showing for each model that we built

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:22.530
the number, this is just
the test set, so we...

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:25.130
I should have mentioned earlier

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:30.130
that we had to manually
find a training set

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:31.950
of mercury stained specimens.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.660
So, it was a lot of hours
of going through images,

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:38.300
and we pulled, we had a set of 10,000

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:41.670
that were mercury stained
and 10,000 that were clean

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.083
that we labeled by hand.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:46.470
And the confusion matrix shows,

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.282
just for a test set,

00:18:48.282 --> 00:18:51.760
which of the stained and un-stained

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:56.760
actual images were predicted
to be stained and un-stained.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.720
So, you would hope that
the diagonal going this way

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:04.983
would be 100%, it's not a 100%.

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:10.030
It did very well, so the
percentage is in the 90s

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:12.950
for both the family identification
and the mercury staining.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.880
So, we're really excited about the fact

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.160
that this worked,

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:19.403
and you will hear what's next for botany

00:19:19.403 --> 00:19:22.250
in a very, very, we've
expanded this greatly

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:24.800
this afternoon, so I won't
talk anymore about that.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:30.330
We'll talk a little bit
about bumblebees as well.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.687
You may say like, "That's pretty random.

00:19:32.687 --> 00:19:34.880
"Plants and bumblebees," but (laughs)

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:36.470
because the Digitization Program Office

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:38.740
has targeted specific collections,

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.250
we've kind of gone with
where the data are right now,

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:44.530
and so the bumblebee project was to

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:48.727
photograph all the specimens
of bumblebees, around 45,000.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:51.836
Bumblebees are all part
of the same genus, bombus.

00:19:51.836 --> 00:19:54.222
And so, all of these were photographed.

00:19:54.222 --> 00:19:57.330
This is just examples
of how the photos look.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:00.290
They're generally just a lateral view.

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.710
Some of them are a dorsal view.

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:07.420
The labels are, as well, in the image,

00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:09.826
and when I looked at the images I thought,

00:20:09.826 --> 00:20:10.917
(laughs)

00:20:10.917 --> 00:20:12.737
"I can't tell these apart at all.

00:20:12.737 --> 00:20:15.620
"I don't know if this is going to work."

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:16.627
Entomologists might say,

00:20:16.627 --> 00:20:18.337
"Well, you know, the
way we tell these apart

00:20:18.337 --> 00:20:22.350
"is very specific banding
patterns and genitalia."

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:24.380
Well, we can't really
see that in these images

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:25.990
very well so I don't know,

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:27.190
but we'll give it a try.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:30.700
The other interesting part of this project

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:32.390
is the transcription was crowdsourced

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:34.760
through the Smithsonian
Transcription Center,

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:36.749
which is where volunteers go online,

00:20:36.749 --> 00:20:38.560
and can transcribe label data

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:40.260
which is a really great way to

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:44.163
get people involved in real research.

00:20:45.769 --> 00:20:50.630
All of our labels, we have
good transcribed data.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.160
So, we didn't really have to go through,

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.510
and make the training set ourselves.

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:56.190
The interesting thing is that

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:01.350
about more than 10,000 were unidentified,

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:05.180
and I talked to the bee
curator at Natural History,

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.180
and he said, "No, that can't be."

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.677
And then, we opened the cabinets and said,

00:21:08.677 --> 00:21:11.260
"Yeah, 10,000 of these are unidentified."

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:15.130
So, the idea being that if our model

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:18.843
can classify to species pretty well,

00:21:18.843 --> 00:21:21.080
we could at least have estimates

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:24.810
of what the other unidentified bees are.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:26.720
We may not be able to put that

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.340
directly back into the collections system,

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.382
but we can at least tell
researchers coming in,

00:21:31.382 --> 00:21:34.127
"You might also be
interested in these specimens

00:21:34.127 --> 00:21:36.570
"because we think they're this species."

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:38.626
So, this didn't show up super well.

00:21:38.626 --> 00:21:40.590
We trained two models so far.

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:44.130
A subgenus model, there
are 15 subgenre of bombus

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.180
that we have in our data set,

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:49.440
and the overall accuracy is above 93%.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:50.680
And for the species model,

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:52.360
this was really interesting,

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:55.270
we're still just starting
out working with this,

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:57.510
but there's 178 species,

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.670
and some of them have 10 images or so.

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.160
And so, I've thrown them all in

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:06.249
even though those are very
small amounts of data.

00:22:06.249 --> 00:22:08.426
It's still overall very accurate.

00:22:08.426 --> 00:22:11.930
The next steps are to
talk to the bee curator

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.530
in more detail about whether

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.088
any of the things that
are more often confused

00:22:16.088 --> 00:22:18.970
are actual taxonomic issues

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.420
that are either mislabeled in the data

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.203
or they are very, very closely related.

00:22:28.627 --> 00:22:30.750
We're also just starting to experiment

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.180
with the activation heat maps

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:34.200
that we can generate from the models

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.960
that allow us to look at which pixels

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.130
are activated in the model,

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.560
and start understanding what the model

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:42.633
is looking at in the bee.

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:46.980
Success in this area
will be really important

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.150
to convincing the biologists that

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:50.320
it's not just a black box,

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.430
that we're actually...

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:53.980
the model is seeing the same

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:55.530
kinds of characters that you're looking at

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:58.293
on your microscope, and it's not so scary.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:02.590
So, now I'll talk a little bit about

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:04.000
the project we're really just starting,

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:06.476
but the Smithsonian
Conservation Biology Institute

00:23:06.476 --> 00:23:10.230
Center for Conservation
and Sustainability,

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:12.870
they work with oil companies mostly

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:16.860
to look at impacts of
installing pipelines,

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:17.973
mostly in the Amazon.

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.910
And, with this project, there is a company

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.130
that's looking to install
this flexible pipeline,

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:29.130
and they are looking to look
at baseline biodiversity

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:32.773
before, during, and
after the installation.

00:23:32.773 --> 00:23:35.540
They've traditionally used camera traps,

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:39.470
and people actually catching
animals, taking DNA.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:44.700
But we're trying a new approach

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:47.270
where fisherman have been given cameras

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:49.990
and given training into
how to photograph fish

00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:51.490
that they catch.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:52.750
We're getting the images,

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.900
and now we will start training a model

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:58.203
to see if we can identify fish to species.

00:24:00.018 --> 00:24:02.870
And as the pipeline gets installed,

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.170
we can have a time series, hopefully,

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:07.220
of what the communities look like

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:09.970
before, during, and after
at different time points,

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:13.250
and then figure out what impacts

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:16.163
the pipeline has on fish communities.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:18.030
That's really exciting

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:19.963
because it has real world impact.

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:24.240
And then, I'll talk briefly about

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.749
two other collaborations we're working on.

00:24:26.749 --> 00:24:29.065
The United States
Holocaust Memorial Museum

00:24:29.065 --> 00:24:32.820
approached us because they have lots

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:35.660
of digital data, mostly scans.

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:37.350
Although, the project we're talking about

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:41.910
is scans of documents without
any metadata about them,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:44.350
just what archive they come from.

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:45.790
So, we are current looking for

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:49.760
a postdoc to lead this project to start

00:24:50.667 --> 00:24:52.190
understanding more about the

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.760
kinds of documents that they have.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.118
So, they have a lot of
diaries, letters, lists,

00:24:56.118 --> 00:24:58.210
birth certificates, marriage certificates.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:02.579
Just all sorts of archival
data around the time

00:25:02.579 --> 00:25:04.810
that the Holocaust was happening,

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:06.840
and making it more
discoverable for researchers

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:09.860
that come to their museum to do research

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:14.160
and in terms of the digital
data don't know where to start.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.793
So, we're hoping to make a dent in that.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:19.590
And the Smithsonian American Women's

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:21.040
History Initiative is another

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.740
pan-Smithsonian initiative
that started last year

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.600
with a large digital component,

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.477
and we're hoping to contribute

00:25:28.477 --> 00:25:32.090
by training machine learning models to

00:25:33.772 --> 00:25:36.590
again, have a better baseline data

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:38.336
about how women have been involved in

00:25:38.336 --> 00:25:41.830
the Smithsonian over time
both in collecting objects

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:44.260
as well as producing artworks.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:48.070
You know, where are the
women in the Smithsonian?

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:53.070
What stories have we missed
in the last 150 years?

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:58.490
So, to that end, we have
some preliminary data

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:00.840
from Smithsonian archives
where we're looking at

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.350
official Smithsonian photographer images

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:05.470
where generally they go through

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:06.870
and identify people that,

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:10.700
let's say for example that
this is an example of a photo.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:12.280
They're really just
interesting on a human level

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:16.040
to look at the photos of what
was going on in the 1970s.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:18.690
In this case, the women
are all identified,

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:20.900
but there are many,
many thousands of images

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:24.030
where people aren't identified.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.936
And in group portraits, more often,

00:26:25.936 --> 00:26:28.630
men are identified and women are left out

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:30.340
for whatever reason.

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:33.520
So, we're hoping to first get a survey

00:26:33.520 --> 00:26:37.140
of how many times women that are labeled

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:41.080
in these images show up in other images

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:42.200
that aren't labeled,

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:45.983
and to start getting some
baseline data about that.

00:26:46.868 --> 00:26:48.714
Here are just some examples.

00:26:48.714 --> 00:26:50.960
There are lots of group
portraits like these

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:53.750
where it's from a meeting
or from something else,

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:55.070
and people aren't labeled.

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:57.930
But maybe we can start making connections

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.940
between people where we have images

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:01.390
where we know where they are.

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:07.290
And also finding completely
outside of knowing who they are

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:14.120
clustering faces as well
in the official photographs

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:18.690
to try to find people who
maybe haven't been described.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:20.320
We don't have their story represented

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:25.320
in any Smithsonian collection or metadata,

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:28.150
but maybe we can start
elevating their stories.

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:32.320
So, the takeaways are

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.440
we've really only just started this work.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.900
There's so much to do.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.470
And right now every, oh
sorry, there's a typo,

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:39.930
every application of machine learning

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:42.596
is a research project given our diverse

00:27:42.596 --> 00:27:45.513
and incomplete, unique data.

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:51.599
So, I made another photo
mosaic of the castle

00:27:51.599 --> 00:27:54.690
with all the portraits data
that I've got from archives,

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.510
so portraits of Smithsonian people

00:27:56.510 --> 00:27:58.473
where we know the people.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.740
I keep thinking who are we missing

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.300
when we're talking about who

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:06.860
has kind of built the Smithsonian,

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:08.970
and so, hoping that we can use these tools

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.680
to elevate new stories
that better represent

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:13.980
the people who actually
visit the Smithsonian.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.210
And so here's my team,

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:20.090
and this work would not
happen without them.

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:24.322
We have from left my
postdoc Mirian Tsuchiya,

00:28:24.322 --> 00:28:26.934
Mike Trizna, and Alex White.

00:28:26.934 --> 00:28:29.410
Alex will be talking later.

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:31.030
Our partners across the Smithsonian,

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:32.380
again this work wouldn't be possible

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:34.383
without all the other Smithsonian units.

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:37.720
And funding, the Smithsonian
Women's Committee,

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:41.145
the Office of the Provost,
and the Office of the CIO.

00:28:41.145 --> 00:28:42.259
Thanks.

00:28:42.259 --> 00:28:45.259
(audience clapping)

00:28:49.191 --> 00:28:51.691
(light music)

